{
  "format": "optibench-workstation",
  "version": 1,
  "meta": {
    "appVersion": "0.1.0-alpha",
    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "Two circuit-driven beam controls. CHOPPER: function generator FG2 produces a 10 Hz sawtooth (0-5 V) that turns a stopped 10-slot chopper wheel through an actuator link (wheel angle = 14.4 deg/V, i.e. two 36 degree slot periods per ramp), so the red laser beam is switched on and off at 20 Hz with a 50 % duty cycle; a 10 % pick-off plate sends a sample to the monitor photodiode, which sees the square wave. GALVO: FG1 sweeps a 2 Hz triangle of +-2.5 V; a second actuator link turns the galvo mirror by 2 deg/V (+-5 degrees mechanical), which swings the reflected beam by twice that angle and paints a 88 mm long line on the screen 250 mm above - a one-axis laser scanner. Try a sine or sawtooth scan, raise the amplitude until the beam leaves the screen, or switch the chopper's motor on (then a static trace shows the 50 % time average).",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "Galvo Scanner & Optical Chopper"
  },
